WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.700
All these years after its release, debates
around Donnie Darko's true meaning rage on.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:10.120
Some elements can be adequately explained,
while others are ambiguous and ripe for interpretation.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:14.080
We're here to offer insight — and lots of
spoilers — on both fronts.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:18.580
Separating Donnie Darko's deeper meaning from
its surface-level narrative is crucial to

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:19.840
understanding the ending.

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:24.800
Superficially, it's the story of wide-eyed
teenager Donnie, portrayed by Jake Gyllenhaal.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.260
Throughout the film, he struggles to fit in
with his peers.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.080
From the get-go, Donnie seems to suffer from
delusions.

00:00:30.189 --> 00:00:34.980
Combined with a track record of unruly behavior,
regular therapy sessions, and medication,

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:38.300
it's heavily implied that Donnie is suffering
from paranoid schizophrenia.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.760
"Donnie is experiencing what is commonly called
a 'daylight hallucination.'"

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:49.120
Beyond the typical challenges of adolescence,
Donnie is plagued by disturbing hallucinations

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.600
of a six-foot rabbit named Frank.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:56.260
He drinks, rebels, and falls in love with
one of his classmates, Gretchen Ross, played

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:57.040
by Jena Malone.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:01.410
He also has a love-hate relationship with
his parents, Rose and Eddie, played by Mary

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:05.950
McDonnell and Holmes Osborne, and his sisters,
Samantha and Elizabeth, played by Daveigh

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:09.420
Chase and Jake Gyllenhaal's real-life sister,
Maggie Gyllenhaal.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.680
But Donnie's hallucinations are part of a
tangent universe where doomsday is coming,

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.380
and time travel is real.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.200
Using this outlandish premise, director Richard
Kelly's cult classic simultaneously tackles

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.960
themes of fate, religion, sacrifice, love,
loss, and loneliness.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.180
Let's take a closer look at The Philosophy
of Time Travel.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:31.780
It's the book given to Donnie by his science
teacher Kenneth Monnitoff,

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.080
The book was written in 1944 by wispy-haired
Roberta Sparrow, portrayed by actress Patience

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:37.600
Cleveland.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.180
Sparrow used to be a science teacher at Donnie's
school, years before she was known as Grandma

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:42.680
Death.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:46.500
The only way to make any sense of the text
is by reading excerpts directly.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.440
"It's called The Philosophy of Time Travel."

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.080
"What does philosophy have to do with time
travel?"

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:54.100
"And guess who wrote it?"

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:59.500
Helpfully, the 2003 Director's Cut DVD included
text from the fictitious book.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.900
The text provides a number of technical terms
relating to the two different dimensions Donnie

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:07.649
Darko takes place in — the Primary Universe
and the Tangent Universe.

00:02:07.649 --> 00:02:09.920
The Primary Universe is the universe we live
in.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:14.960
The Tangent Universe is a carbon copy of the
Primary Universe, which forms when there's

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.200
a glitch in the Fourth Dimension of Time.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:18.380
"Duh."

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:18.880
"Heh."

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:23.640
"Excuse me, no 'Duh' is a product of fear."

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:30.020
In the film's timeline, this glitch occurs
shortly before the jet engine crashes into

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:33.360
the Darko household on October 2, 1988.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.260
That's when Donnie first sees Frank in his
dreams.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:41.120
After saving Donnie's life by guiding him
away from his house, Frank makes a prophetic

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:46.500
warning: In 28 days , six hours, 42 minutes
and 12 seconds, the world will end.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:48.640
"Why?"

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:57.480
Here's the reason why: Tangent Universes are
"highly unstable" and only exist for a few

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:57.980
weeks.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.660
Eventually, they collapse on themselves.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.760
According to The Philosophy of Time Travel,
they form

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:07.180
"... a black hole within the Primary Universe,
capable of destroying all existence."

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:12.450
The events in the Tangent Universe that occur
from October 2 to October 31 transpire away

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.500
from the Primary Universe's timeline.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.780
In effect, the Primary Universe is "on hold"
during the Tangent Universe's existence, which

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:22.260
explains the glitch in the Fourth Dimension
of Time.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:23.360
Got all that?

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:26.460
Fortunately, there's a way the universe can
be saved.

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:30.500
The Artifact — which, in this case, is the
jet engine — is the key.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:34.940
The Tangent Universe is a like-for-like copy
of the Primary Universe.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:39.540
The Artifact is an exception that materializes
spontaneously after a Tangent Universe is

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:40.040
formed.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.820
This object, made of metal, is erroneously
copied twice, and it's the first sign that

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:47.420
a Tangent Universe has formed.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:52.280
It's the only difference between the two universes
and it disrupts the natural order of things.

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:55.240
"I'm not following you."

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.439
You see, in order for the Tangent Universe
to collapse safely after its twenty-eight-day

00:04:00.439 --> 00:04:05.540
lifespan, it must be identical to the Primary
Universe — so the Artifact needs to leave

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:08.240
the Tangent Universe before it can collapse.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.320
The only way to remove the Artifact from the
universe is to send it through a time portal

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.020
back to the Primary Universe.

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:20.360
To save the universe, someone living inside
the Tangent Universe must transfer the Artifact

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:23.140
back in time to October 2nd, 1988.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:27.440
To clarify, there are actually three jet engines
in Donnie Darko.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.900
Jet Engine 1 belongs in the Primary Universe,
still attached to its airplane.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:36.820
The Tangent Universe forms, containing Jet
Engine 2 and Jet Engine 3.

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:41.440
Jet Engine 2 is correctly attached to the
duplicated airplane, which Donnie's mother

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:43.100
and sister travel on later in the film.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:48.780
Jet Engine 3 is the anomaly: With nowhere
to go in the Tangent Universe, it crashes

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:53.420
into the Darko family's house — but Frank
manages to save Donnie.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:58.380
Jet Engine 2 is later torn from the airplane
and sent back to the Primary Universe, killing

00:04:58.380 --> 00:04:58.880
Donnie.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:03.900
By the end of the film, the Primary Universe
contains the identical Jet Engine 1 and Jet

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:04.520
Engine 2.

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:09.360
But because the Primary Universe is stable,
it's able to withstand the anomaly of two

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:10.360
identical objects.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:15.440
Donnie Darko is chosen as the Living Receiver,
and his mission is to return the Artifact

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:16.640
to the Primary Universe.

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.520
Why was Donnie chosen?

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:21.750
Well, as The Philosophy of Time Travel conveniently
states:

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:24.680
"...no one knows how or why a Receiver will
be chosen..."

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:26.820
"You call this clarity?"

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:31.720
The Philosophy of Time Travel states that
the Living Receiver is blessed with "Fourth

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:37.220
Dimensional Powers," including, quote, "enhanced
strength, telekinesis, mind control, and the

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:39.280
ability to conjure fire and water."

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:42.920
Knowing this helps explain how Donnie was
able to succeed in his mission.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:47.680
But these powers come with a cost — the
Living Receiver also experiences hallucinations

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.000
and terrifying dreams.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:53.480
"Who is he going to kill, Donnie?"

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:54.900
"I can see him right now!"

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:58.480
It's not only the Living Receiver who's affected.

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:02.840
Those who are close to Donnie subconsciously
guide him along on his mission, often acting

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:03.460
erratically.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:07.500
Other than Donnie, all the other characters
fall into one of two categories: the Manipulated

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:11.040
Living, or the Manipulated Dead.

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:16.480
Most characters in Donnie Darko fall into
the first category, "the Manipulated Living."

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:20.130
They have an important job but know little
of it, subconsciously helping Donnie along

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:20.640
his journey.

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:26.780
"I guess people are just born with tragedy
in their blood…"

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:32.520
Donnie's fate is to kill Frank, portrayed
by actor James Duval, by shooting him through

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:33.100
the eye.

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:36.440
After Frank is killed, he becomes one of the
Manipulated Dead.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:37.980
This is crucial.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:42.760
Without Frank as a guide, Donnie would have
no warning, and no indication of his role

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:44.000
in saving the universe.

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:47.820
Anyone killed in the Tangent Universe becomes
one of the Manipulated Dead.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:51.980
Their in-universe death makes them more powerful
than the Manipulating Living.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:56.520
This is presumably because their spirits move
beyond the material world to a metaphysical

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:00.280
dimension, while they remain alive in the
suspended Primary Universe.

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:04.600
They're capable of time travel and, unlike
the living, have conscious knowledge of the

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:06.520
threat of universal annihilation.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:08.400
Frank's influence is obvious.

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:10.460
"Why do you make me flood the school?"

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:15.140
"You're in great danger."

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:19.900
He commands Donnie to flood the school and
burn down Jim Cunningham's house.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:23.480
Every action that Frank encourages has very
significant consequences.

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:28.020
Kenneth Monnitoff helps Donnie discover his
purpose by discussing wormholes, portals,

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.040
and the feasibility of time travel before.

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:34.900
Fellow teacher Karen Pomeroy, portrayed by
Drew Barrymore, plays Cupid between Gretchen

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:37.540
and Donnie, giving Gretchen this bizarre instruction:

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:41.160
Sit next to the boy you think is the cutest.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:42.640
Quiet!"

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:48.300
After she's fired, she opts for more overt
tactics, like writing "Cellar Door" on the

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:48.800
chalkboard.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:52.380
Well, after leaving a Halloween party, Donnie
opens a cellar door.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:54.400
"Cellar door!"

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:55.960
"What?"

00:07:57.100 --> 00:08:00.920
But after opening the door, he bumps into
bullies Seth and Ricky.

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:02.240
Why are they there?

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:04.700
Because they are among the Manipulated Living.

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:09.900
Once they've dragged Donnie and Gretchen outdoors,
the pieces are in place for Frank's arrival.

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:14.120
Frank swerves his car to avoid Roberta Sparrow
and ends up running over Gretchen.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:19.340
The events in the Tangent Universe imply something,
or someone, is orchestrating everything behind

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:19.840
the scenes.

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:25.000
Graffiti outside Donnie's school reads, "They
made me do it," but who are "they"?

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:30.360
In the DVD commentary, director Richard Kelly
explains that the force, in essence, is God.

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:34.440
Throughout the film, God is represented in
the form of a glowing tunnel.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:37.540
During therapy, Donnie refers to the tunnels
as:

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:39.800
"Workers… assigned to each one of us."

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:44.260
These "workers" first appear In Donnie's house,
leading Donnie to his parents' room, where

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:46.740
he finds the gun he eventually uses to kill
Frank.

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:50.220
During therapy, Donnie opens up about his
fear of dying alone.

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:55.220
Without discovering God, it sounds highly
unlikely he would have ever sacrificed himself.

00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:58.800
"The search for God is absurd?"

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:02.120
"It is if everyone dies alone."

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:08.200
However, Donnie's direct communication with
God, in the form of the aforementioned workers,

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:08.800
comforts him.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:13.120
Donnie utters a phrase to Seth when Frank's
speeding car saves him from the knife-wielding

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:13.680
bully:

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:16.660
Deus ex machina."

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:17.580
"What did you just say?"

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.810
"Deus ex machina" is in reference to a plot
device used in fiction when an unexpected

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:24.880
event saves a hopeless situation.

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:30.580
However, its translation "God from the machine"
has a different implication: Donnie knows

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:32.860
Frank's arrival is part of God's plan.

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:36.200
As they take a stroll through the park, Gretchen
asks Donnie:

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:41.800
"What if you could go back in time, and take
all those hours of pain and darkness, and

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:43.860
replace them with something better?"

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:49.300
That comment seems innocent at the time, but
remember: Gretchen is one of the Manipulated

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:52.230
Dead, since she dies in the Tangent Universe.

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:54.060
She was nudging Donnie towards his fate.

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:59.620
"What if you could go back in time and take
all those hours of pain and darkness, and

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:01.600
replace them with something better?"

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:07.160
After shooting Frank, Donnie carries Gretchen's
body to his house, stops off to kiss a sleeping

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:10.780
Elizabeth on the head, then drives the family
car to Carpathian Ridge.

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:13.260
An ominous twister-like cloud forms.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:14.900
But what happens next?

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:19.680
As the plane his mother and sister have both
boarded travels towards the cloud, Donnie

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:20.300
chuckles.

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:24.980
On first viewing, it's unclear that he's using
telekinesis, a power given to him as the Living

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:29.240
Receiver, to rip the engine from the plane
and send it through the portal, back in time

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:30.340
to the Primary Universe.

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:35.320
The natural order of the universe is thus
restored, which means the Tangent Universe

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:38.460
can collapse safely, as Frank's countdown
reaches zero.

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:42.500
This collapse is symbolized by the past 28
days played in reverse.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:46.460
Donnie's voiceover reads aloud from the letter
he sent to Roberta Sparrow:

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:53.600
"I hope that when the world comes to an end,
I can breathe a sigh of relief, because there

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:54.960
will be so much to look forward to."

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:59.310
The narrative then returns to the Primary
Universe, last seen at the beginning of the

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:01.280
film before Donnie is woken up by Frank.

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:04.300
Donnie sits up in bed, hysterically laughing.

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:09.620
Then Jet Engine 2, sent back in time from
October 30, crashes into his room and Donnie

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:10.600
is killed.

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:12.740
"Donnie Darko.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:14.880
What the hell kind of name is that?

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:16.160
It's like some sort of superhero or something."

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:19.460
"What makes you think I'm not?"

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:22.700
In the end, Donnie Darko is a superhero story.

00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:27.300
When he wakes up in bed after successfully
returning the Artifact, Donnie laughs because

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:30.420
he's saved the universe, and saved the lives
of Gretchen and Frank.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:35.170
His impact reaches further into the Primary
Universe, too, with characters later recollecting

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:37.380
key moments from the Tangent Universe.

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:41.560
In The Philosophy of Time Travel, it's said
that when the Manipulated Living and Dead

00:11:41.570 --> 00:11:43.310
wake up in the Primary Universe,

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:45.980
"they are often haunted by the experience
in their dreams."

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:51.560
In other words, they experience a vague recollection
of events living on in their subconscious.

00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:56.160
In one of the most fitting choices for movie
soundtracks, an acoustic cover of Tears for

00:11:56.170 --> 00:12:01.090
Fears' "Mad World" plays, which notably includes
the line: "The dreams in which I'm dying are

00:12:01.090 --> 00:12:02.240
the best I've ever had."

00:12:02.650 --> 00:12:07.300
It's an apt reference to Donnie Darko, the
troubled teen who sacrificed his life to protect

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:11.730
the universe, and whose heroism is only vaguely
remembered in the dreams of all the people

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:12.260
he saved.

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:16.610
Tune in next time, when Looper weighs in on
the many merits of performance-art troupe

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:17.940
Sparkle Motion.

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:22.280
"Sometimes I doubt your commitment to Sparkle
Motion."

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:25.980
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:29.340
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
stuff are coming soon.

00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:33.320
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

